  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none mb-12">
    <h2>1. The Particle Gradation Science: ASTM C33 vs ASTM C144</h2>
    <p>
      In geotechnical engineering, aggregate stability depends on <strong>grain size distribution (gradation)</strong> and <strong>particle angularity</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ASTM C33 Washed Concrete Sand:</strong> Consists of sharp, jagged, angular silica sand grains ranging from coarse (No. 4 sieve, 4.75 mm) down to fine (No. 100 sieve). It contains less than 1% fine silt or clay. When compacted, these angular grains interlock mechanically like micro-gravel, creating immense internal friction and high shear strength while allowing water to freely percolate through without washing away.</li>
      <li><strong>ASTM C144 Mason Sand:</strong> Screened extremely fine with rounded, smooth micro-particles intended for mixing buttery smooth brick mortar. It lacks coarse angular grains. Under repeated pedestrian or vehicular traffic, smooth round grains slip past one another, behaving like millions of microscopic ball bearings. Pavers resting on mason sand immediately shift, slide, and develop deep ruts.</li>
      <li><strong>Stone Dust (Screenings / Quarry Waste):</strong> The byproduct of limestone crushing. It contains a high percentage of ultra-fine rock flour (passing the No. 200 sieve). When wet, stone dust turns into an impermeable paste that traps water beneath the pavers, leading to freeze-thaw heaving and severe efflorescence.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Bedding Material Direct Comparison Matrix</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border border-slate-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-slate-100 text-slate-800 font-bold border-b border-slate-200">
