    <h2>3. The 1-Inch Uncompacted Thickness Rule</h2>
    <p>
      The Interlocking Concrete Pavement Institute strictly mandates a nominal bedding layer thickness of <strong>1.0 inch (25 mm)</strong>, with an allowable tolerance of &plusmn; 3/8 inch.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Why You Must Never Exceed 1.5 Inches:</strong> Amateurs often use thick sand (2 to 3 inches) to compensate for poorly graded, unlevel crushed stone bases. An unconfined sand layer thicker than 1.5 inches cannot resist lateral shear forces; walking or driving on it pushes sand out from under high-load spots into adjacent joints, creating undulating waves across the pavement.</li>
      <li><strong>Why Bedding Sand Is Never Pre-Compacted:</strong> You must screed bedding sand loose. The initial compaction occurs <em>after</em> the pavers are laid on top. As the vibratory plate compactor runs across the pavers, it presses the bricks down into the loose sand bed, forcing approximately 1/2 inch of sand up into the bottom of the paver joints, achieving the critical "interlock" that gives segmental pavement its strength.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. The Efflorescence Trap of Limestone Stone Dust</h2>
    <p>
      Stone dust contains high concentrations of soluble calcium salts. Rainwater filters through paver joints and pools in the impermeable stone dust layer. As the water evaporates upward through the porous concrete paver units, it dissolves the calcium hydroxide, which reacts with atmospheric carbon dioxide at the surface to form <strong>calcium carbonate</strong> (insoluble white efflorescence salt). This creates unsightly chalky white staining that cannot be washed away with water.
    </p>
  </article>

